## ===========================================================================
## RED GATE ESTATE — Book 11 (`story.ni:10008-10587`), Chapter 8, with the
## estate front's unlock (`story.ni:4858-4901`) and Book 4's Chapter 9
## trigger (`story.ni:5262`).
##
## The change document ("Chapter 8 — Red Gate Estate", 2026-09-02)
## authorizes Book 11 whole, from Shannon coming through the privy window
## after her to her goodbye in Lord's Market and Jack's step into Sandler &
## Sons: the park wall, the street, the gates and the brass key, the empty
## house, the furnace and the fire, the box of letters, the bed, the bath,
## the dress and the mirror. Every line is Gentry's; conversations are STUBS
## under the standing rule. Shannon's company on the streets and in Maiden
## House's back alley is layered onto `maiden-house.chord`,
## `commerce-street.chord` and `lords-market.chord`, gated on `the company`.
##
## THE RULINGS (change document, Chapter 8):
##   * EXTENT — the window to Shannon's goodbye in the square and the step
##     into Sandler & Sons; Chapter IX's row begins as the company ends
##     (`the company becomes done`).
##   * THE STREET — new content, fear without teeth: men visible on East
##     Commerce Street when the two drop from the park wall, and Shannon
##     hurries her to the gates. ONE placeholder on that arrival; the house
##     stays silent, empty, unclocked, as the source has it.
##   * THE BATH AND THE DRESS — the presentation flips, as the source has it:
##     from the mirror on the public reads her as a young lady. `Jack is
##     clean` is that state (`secret-letter.story`); the mirror text and the
##     faucet monologue carry as written.
##   * THE WALK OUT — new content: the men are still on the street when the
##     gates open again and she walks past them in the dress with Shannon at
##     her elbow. ONE placeholder on the clean arrival on East Commerce
##     Street, before Shannon's "I suppose we should go see Mrs. Sandler
##     now"; the walk east and the goodbye carry as written.
##   * WHO SEES HER — Shannon sees: every "Jacqueline" of hers carries. ONE
##     line converts, per §3g: at the portrait, the source's "Jack, dear, you
##     have his eyes" is "Jacqueline, dear" (gap 4 notes the alternative).
##     From the mirror on, the words no longer tell.
##
## STUBS TAKING THE SOURCE'S PATH: `talk to shannon` outside the house
## prints the SH13-SH16 TODO; inside, the source's own refusal.
##
## GAPS (change document): the street beats (two placeholders); the two dead
## rooms — the Dining Hall and Kitchen are commented out in the source and
## are not built (the Great Hall is north of the Entrance Hall, as the source
## compiles); "Estelle" in the third letter carries as written; Shannon's
## converted line.
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * The source's room names "Second Floor Landing", "Third Floor Landing"
##     and "Master Bedroom" — a room's name is story-wide and Black Gate
##     Estate holds those three, so this house's are `the Red Gate …`.
##   * `rub`/`wipe` on the dust, the sheets and the desk — no rubbing action;
##     `clean`/`wipe the target` reach the kitchen's `washing` story action
##     and the desk answers through a trait.
##   * The "Shannon follows silently / close by" line — the source prints it
##     on a walked move while the tension is still unimportant, which in
##     practice is only the walk into the square at the end, where the
##     goodbye speaks instead; the follower clause moves her and says nothing.
##   * `wear dress` reaches Chapter 1's escape dress, which has no location
##     (GH #374); the day dress answers to `wear day dress`.
##   * The furnace's contents listing and the box's contents listing ride
##     the platform's container reports.
##   * The love letters' `[first time]` tail sits with the documents' first
##     look, where the source first reveals them; `x letters` afterward is
##     their text alone.
##   * `unlock gates with wire` ("too sturdy") — a clause cannot read the
##     instrument; without the key the gates are simply locked. The bare
##     `unlock gates` asks for the key (the source supplies it).
##   * `enter tub` and `switch on faucets` — the platform refuses to enter
##     or switch scenery before a clause can act; `bathe`, `take a bath` and
##     `turn faucets` are the ways in.
##   * Standing on the desk puts her on it, as the platform's entering does;
##     the source steps off by itself, the port needs `get off`.
##   * The source's `select theme` changes, the image rules, the
##     `pause`-free heading.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## SHANNON'S COMPANY (`story.ni:10014-10071`) — the state the streets and
## the widow read. `pending` until the window; `escorting` from the alley to
## the square; `done` at her goodbye.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the company
  aka shannon's company
  proper
  states: pending, escorting, done

  Widow Shannon's company.

## `story.ni:10023`, the window (Chapter VIII's opening; the raid's file
## speaks it as she climbs out).

define phrase shannon-through-the-window
  You pull yourself through the window and once again clamber up into the
  alley. You can hear a commotion from the direction of Lord's Market; it
  looks as though the Baron's mercenaries are still out searching the streets
  for you, but they'll double back and put a watch on Maiden House soon
  enough.

  There is a scrabbling, grunting sound behind you, and you turn to see
  Shannon trying to push her way through the window. Confused but not
  wanting to just stand there and watch her struggle, you give her a hand
  up.

  'Fiona thought I should maybe come with you,' she says, after she's
  finished smoothing her skirts and catching her breath. 'In case you needed
  any help.'
end phrase

## `story.ni:10046-10053`

define phrase shannon-cut-through-the-park
  Shannon lays a hand on your elbow. 'Wait, Jacqueline,' she whispers. 'The
  mercenaries will be watching the entrance. We'd best cut through the park.
  I'll help you climb the wall.'
end phrase

define phrase shannon-boosts-you-over
  Shannon laces her fingers together and gives you a boost up to the top of
  the wall; then you reach down and help pull her up. You both drop down
  onto the grass on the other side, and then it's a quick sprint across the
  open space of the park. Fortunately, no one is about after the awful
  spectacle in Lord's Market, and you make it to the relative safety of East
  Commerce Street without anyone spotting you.
end phrase

## `story.ni:10057-10061`

define phrase shannon-stay-away-from-market
  'We should stay away from Lord's Market for a while, I think,' says
  Shannon. 'Fossville's men will still be looking for you.'
end phrase

define phrase shannon-dont-wander
  'We shouldn't wander the streets any more than we have to,' Shannon says.
  'Fossville's men will still be looking for you.'
end phrase

