## THE GREAT HALL (`story.ni:10163-10218`): the portrait, the furniture, the
## sheets. The `[first time]` paragraph is the first-visit description.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the Great Hall
  a room
  aka hall, great hall, large hall, long room
  south to the Entrance Hall
  up to the Red Gate Second Floor Landing
  down to the Cellar
  first time
    This room runs nearly the entire length of this floor, but its size only
    draws attention to its emptiness. The walls are bare plaster; what little
    furniture remains is stacked in the corners and covered with dusty
    sheets.

    You can't help but imagine how this place once was, in its full
    vibrancy. The cream of the city once filled this room, entertained and
    served by the Duke of Toresal — your father. They respected him, honored
    him, even loved him. And now it's all gone.

    A wide stairway sweeps up to the second floor, while a narrower stairway
    leads down to a cellar. The foyer lies south. {portrait-on-the-wall}

  This room runs nearly the entire length of this floor, but its size only
  draws attention to its emptiness. The walls are bare plaster; what little
  furniture remains is stacked in the corners and covered with dusty sheets.
  A wide stairway sweeps up to the second floor, while a narrower stairway
  leads down to a cellar. The foyer lies south. {portrait-on-the-wall}

## The portrait's paragraph (`story.ni:10171`), once each way.

define phrase portrait-on-the-wall, stopping
  Whoever took most of the furniture away for some reason decided to leave a
  large portrait of the Duke himself hanging on the far wall.
or
  The portrait of the Duke stares down at you from the far wall.
end phrase

## The portrait (`story.ni:10171-10186`): Shannon's line after the first
## look — CONVERTED, the ruling: "Jacqueline, dear" for the source's "Jack,
## dear" (Shannon sees; §3g).

create the portrait of the Duke
  aka portrait, painting, picture, canvas, portrait of the old duke, duke, old duke, father, my father, your father, duke's eyes, eyes
  scenery
  in the Great Hall

  The Duke's features are strong and stern, yet softened by kindness, and
  although the canvas is faded with the years, his eyes are still a rich,
  vivid blue. In the painting he is smiling slightly, but as he stares out at
  the empty ruin of his home — at you, his lost, beleaguered, and only heir —
  he seems weighed down by sadness.

