  You can't reach the wooden box from where you're standing.
end phrase

define phrase box-nestled-papers-first
  Nestled in the box's velvet-lined interior are bundles of papers — letters
  and documents of the old Duke.
end phrase

define phrase box-nestled-papers
  Nestled in the box's velvet-lined interior are bundles of papers, letters
  and documents of the old Duke.
end phrase

define phrase shannon-what-a-treasure
  'The Duke's old correspondences,' breathes Shannon. 'What a treasure,
  Jacqueline!'
end phrase

define phrase shannon-keep-those
  'No, you should keep those, Jacqueline,' says Shannon firmly.
end phrase

create the collected documents
  aka documents, papers, legal documents, financial records, records, bundles of papers, bundles, correspondence, correspondences, stack
  plural
  in the wooden box

  Some of them are legal documents indicating the disposition of the old
  Lord's estate. There are also financial records of the city's Treasury,
  details of various funds and improvements he planned to implement before
  he died. There are letters from noblemen and noblewomen from every corner
  of Miradania, addressing the Duke in terms of the utmost deference and
  respect.

  At the bottom of the stack are several letters written on small, worn
  parchment.

  after the player examining
    first time
      phrase your-mothers-letters
      move the old love letters to the wooden box
  end after

  on the player taking
    refuse leave-them-in-the-box
  end on

  on the player showing
    refuse shannon-close-to-tears
  end on

## The three letters (`story.ni:10386-10390`) and the realization — on the
## documents' first reading, where the source first reveals them.

define phrase your-mothers-letters
  The penmanship is clumsy, like the way yours looked when Fiona was first
  teaching you your letters. Each is short, no more than a few scribbled
  sentences:

  My Lord. I had some time to myself in the garden and thought of you. I
  hope to see you again soon if your duties will let you get away. You can
  see I've not put my name to this like you asked but you know who it is.
  Your Love.

  Dear Love. When will I see you again? Your duties keep you too long from
  me. But that is why I love you so, you are a good man who never forsakes
  his duty. Come see me soon. Your Love.

  My precious Duke. I pressed Estelle with subtle questions but I am certain
  she knows nothing. I am discreet and would never give you cause to worry.
  Our secret is safe. Your Love.

  Your mother's words, you realize. Secret letters that she sent to your
  father, before you were born. He would have had no pictures of her, of
  course, nor any documents containing clues to her identity. These pieces
  of paper are all you will ever have of her.
end phrase

create the old love letters
  aka letters, love letters, old letters, mother's letters, small letters, worn parchment, scribbled letters, unsigned letters
  plural

  The penmanship is clumsy, like the way yours looked when Fiona was first
  teaching you your letters. Each is short, no more than a few scribbled
  sentences:

  My Lord. I had some time to myself in the garden and thought of you. I
  hope to see you again soon if your duties will let you get away. You can
  see I've not put my name to this like you asked but you know who it is.
  Your Love.

  Dear Love. When will I see you again? Your duties keep you too long from
  me. But that is why I love you so, you are a good man who never forsakes
  his duty. Come see me soon. Your Love.

  My precious Duke. I pressed Estelle with subtle questions but I am certain
  she knows nothing. I am discreet and would never give you cause to worry.
  Our secret is safe. Your Love.

  on the player taking
    refuse leave-them-in-the-box
  end on

  on the player showing
    refuse shannon-close-to-tears
  end on

define phrase leave-them-in-the-box
  Better to leave them in the box for now. If you take them out, they could
  get scattered and lost.
end phrase

define phrase shannon-close-to-tears, stopping
  Shannon looks close to tears as you show her the letters. 'Your father was
  such a compassionate man,' she whispers. 'I just think it's terrible that
  he was forced to hide away everything that he truly loved.'
or
  Shannon looks close to tears as you show her the letters.
end phrase

