## THE BATH (`story.ni:10540-10551`, the ruling: the presentation flips):
## the faucets' turn carries it (a trait), and the bare forms — `bathe`,
## `take a bath`, `wash myself` — are a story action with the same body.
## `enter tub` cannot be the way in: the platform refuses to enter scenery
## before a clause can act, an enterable tub would keep her in it, and a
## literal `enter tub` shape would shadow the laundry's washtub; the tub's
## own clause carries the cold and once-only refusals. Bare `wash` belongs to
## the kitchen's `wash the target`. Jack wears nothing by now (the jail took it all),
## so the source's garment sweep has nothing to do; the old clothes appear on
## the floor and the day dress in her hands, and she is `clean`.

create the bathtub
  aka tub, bath, basin, granite basin, stone basin, huge basin, granite tub, block of granite, lip
  scenery
  in the Bathroom

  The basin is carved from a single block of granite, polished smooth as
  glass. It looks almost big enough to swim in.

  on the player entering
    refuse when the furnace is cold: water-freezing
    refuse when Jack is clean: once-is-enough
  end on

  on the player taking
    refuse when the furnace is cold: water-freezing
    refuse when Jack is clean: once-is-enough
  end on

define action bathing
  grammar
    bathe
    bathe myself
    wash myself
    take a bath
    take bath
    have a bath
    have a wash
    get washed
    get bathed
  the player must be in the Bathroom: nowhere-to-bathe-stub
  refuse when the furnace is cold: water-freezing
  refuse when Jack is clean: once-is-enough
  phrase the-bath
  move the old clothes to the Bathroom
  move the daydress to the player
  change Jack to clean


## DAVID: the source's bathing anywhere but the bathroom falls to Inform's
## own washing default; one line, yours.

define phrase nowhere-to-bathe-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — bathing anywhere but the Duke's bathroom: no
  source line exists; David's.)
end phrase

define phrase water-freezing
  You turn on the faucets, thinking how nice it would be to take a bath — but
  the water is freezing! No matter how far you turn the 'hot' faucet, the
  water is as cold as if it had been drawn from a deep well.

  'The furnace must be off,' muses Shannon. 'Perhaps we can get it started
  again.'
end phrase

define phrase once-is-enough
  Once is enough for today.
end phrase

define phrase the-bath
  You plug the drain and begin filling the enormous tub with water.

  Shannon nods with approval but says nothing; somehow she seems to know what
  you were thinking. By the time you've gotten out of your sodden clothes and
  into the water, she's managed to scare up a cake of soap from somewhere.
  She smiles as she hands it to you.

  The water is scalding hot and exquisite. You rub the soap into your
  reddening skin, feeling everything wash clean away. Not just the dirt and
  the sweat, but everything: the fear and confusion of being hunted for two
  days straight; the moldy tunnel under Lord's Keep; the prison; the sewers.
  Even your grief and guilt over Bobby's death — it doesn't go away
  completely, not all of it, but it's not so raw any more, not so sharp.

  You dry off with a sheet pulled from the furniture. As you reach for your
  old clothes, Shannon stops you and holds out a dress.

  'Where in the world—?' you ask.

  'The Duke's wife was a little lady, just about your size, really,' Shannon
  says. 'Not beautiful, but handsome in her way. And good taste in clothes.'
  She puts the dress into your hands.
end phrase

## The faucets (`story.ni:10480-10530`): the first look while she is still
## dirty is the monologue (the ruling: it carries as written); turning them
## while dirty is the bath.

define trait faucet-bath
  on the player turning
    refuse when Jack is clean: water-gushes
    refuse when the furnace is cold: water-freezing
    phrase the-bath
    move the old clothes to the Bathroom
    move the daydress to the player
    change Jack to clean
  end on
end trait

create the faucets
  aka faucet, taps, tap, wheels, wheel, spout, curved spout, pipes, pipe, plumbing, water pipes, running water, water, devices, device, strange devices, brass fittings, fittings, hot faucet, cold faucet
  scenery, plural
  faucet-bath
  in the Bathroom
  states: strange, understood

  The faucets are made of brass and ivory and protrude from carved fittings
  in the wall, at about waist height. The curved spout is flanked by two
  wheels: one for cold water, the other (though you can scarcely imagine it)
  for hot.

  on the player examining
    phrase faucets-first-look when the faucets is strange and Jack is not clean
    change the faucets to understood when the faucets is strange
  end on

  on the player drinking
    refuse faucet-water-metallic
  end on

  on the player switching_on
    refuse when Jack is clean: water-gushes
    refuse when the furnace is cold: water-freezing
    refuse turn-the-wheels
  end on

  on the player switching_off
    refuse faucets-already-off
  end on

define phrase faucets-first-look
  The devices are made of brass and ivory and protrude from carved fittings
  in the wall, at about waist height. They consist of a curved spout flanked
  by two hand-sized, spoked wheels...

  Suddenly you figure it out — these are faucets! Running-water pipes, or
  'plumbing' as some call them, are a very recent invention, incredibly rare
  in Toresal. You've never actually seen faucets up close before.

  'Oh, a bath with water-pipes! What a luxury,' exclaims Shannon. 'I've
  never had a bath that I didn't have to fill myself from a kettle. Back and
  forth and back and forth — it's either that or take it cold. Kind of takes
  the relaxation out of it, if you ask me,' she sighs ruefully. 'The Lord
  Duke was a man of taste and distinction on top of everything else. True
  nobility, he was.'

  And it occurs to you, as you look at your distorted reflection in the
  golden, curving pipes, you've never had a bath with running water, either.
  In fact, you can't remember the last time you had any sort of bath, not
  even the quick spit-baths in a wooden tub full of tepid, cloudy water that
  are the norm in Maiden House. You've spent your life coated with the grime
  of the streets more often than not, reeking of sweat and alleyways, hardly
  noticing it because the smell is around you constantly, and around
  everyone you know.

  But, you realize for the first time, that's not really who you are. You
  are the Duke's daughter, a child of nobility. By all rights, you should
  have been sleeping on feather mattresses, smelling of rosewater. This
  luxury is your heritage... and you've never known a single day of it in
  your life.
end phrase

define phrase water-gushes
  A groaning sound reverberates behind the walls, and then a spout of clean,
  clear water gushes out of the spout and splashes into the tub.
end phrase

define phrase faucet-water-metallic
  You turn the faucet on and cup your hand under the water to drink. It
  tastes cold and metallic.
end phrase

